<!--
  The three approved palettes.

  These are RAW VALUES, and their names say which palette they belong to on
  purpose — that is what they are. The *roles* live in `attrs.xml`, and the
  mapping from role to value lives in `themes.xml`. Nothing outside those two
  files may name a colour from here.

  Twelve values per palette are OWNER-APPROVED: the viewport, the base chrome,
  the floating material, the precision surface, the hairline, the two text
  weights, the accent, the selected-at-rest surface, and the warning / error /
  success verdicts. Everything else in a palette is derived from those twelve —
  a control fill lifted off the surface under it, a recessed field, a pressed
  state — and is authored as a NEIGHBOUR of an approved value rather than as a
  new opinion.

  TWO of the twelve were LIGHTENED, on instruction, because they were measured
  below the accessibility target on the surfaces they are actually drawn on.
  `*_text_secondary` carries field captions, section headings, slider labels and
  the active body's name; `*_text_error` carries every refusal the product
  reports. They measured 3.93 / 3.87 / 3.59 and 3.15 / 3.68 / 2.49 against the
  precision surface — a refusal a user cannot read is a refusal that did not
  happen. Every one now clears 4.5:1 against all six grounds either role is ever
  drawn on: the viewport, the chrome surface, the floating material, the
  precision surface, a control fill and a field well. The hue of each is kept —
  warm grey stays warm, the error stays red — so the appearance still reads as
  the same appearance. UILR1-11 measures this and will fail if it drifts back.

  All three are dark. That is the appearance set, not a default with variants:
  the product is a viewport-first modelling tool and every one of its grounds is
  chosen so a neutral clay render reads as lit. Warm Graphite leans red, Neutral
  Charcoal leans blue-grey, and Light Charcoal is the lightest ground the set
  allows.

  One accent across all three, so the product still reads as itself in every
  appearance: the same #4C8FD6, meaning "primary commit" or "focused field" and
  nothing else. A SELECTION is a different, much quieter answer — see
  `attrs.xml` on why those are two roles and not one.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- =====================================================================
         PALETTE 01 — WARM GRAPHITE. The product default.

         A warm dark studio ground: red leads blue by five points, which is
         enough to keep a neutral clay body from looking cold and far short of
         brown.
         ===================================================================== -->
    <!-- APPROVED -->
    <color name="p1_viewport_background">#FF302E2B</color>
    <color name="p1_chrome_surface">#FF3A3835</color>
    <color name="p1_surface_floating">#FF46433F</color>
    <color name="p1_surface_precision">#FF403D39</color>
    <color name="p1_chrome_hairline">#FF565149</color>
    <color name="p1_text_primary">#FFF4F1EB</color>
    <color name="p1_text_secondary">#FFB6B1A9</color>
    <color name="p1_accent">#FF4C8FD6</color>
    <color name="p1_selected_rest">#FF52504A</color>
    <color name="p1_text_measure">#FFE0A84B</color>
    <color name="p1_text_error">#FFE8A093</color>
    <color name="p1_text_success">#FF7FB388</color>

    <!-- DERIVED. The floating material at 94 %: "glass" is faked with tone and
         opacity, never by sampling the viewport — a SurfaceView cannot be blurred
         behind, and pretending otherwise would be a promise the platform breaks. -->
    <color name="p1_surface_floating_translucent">#F046433F</color>
    <!-- A control fill, lifted just clear of the precision surface it sits on. -->
    <color name="p1_control_surface">#FF45423D</color>
    <color name="p1_control_surface_pressed">#FF565249</color>
    <color name="p1_control_border">#FF565149</color>
    <!-- A field is RECESSED rather than raised: a well you type into. -->
    <color name="p1_field_surface">#FF34322F</color>
    <color name="p1_selected_rest_pressed">#FF5C5A53</color>
    <color name="p1_accent_border">#734C8FD6</color>
    <color name="p1_primary_fill_pressed">#FF6BA3DF</color>
    <!-- Ink, not white. #4C8FD6 is a bright accent: white on it is 3.4:1 and a
         near-black ink is 5.5:1, so the one act that changes the model carries a
         label that is actually legible. -->
    <color name="p1_text_on_primary">#FF12110F</color>
    <color name="p1_text_disabled">#FF7A746C</color>
    <color name="p1_slider_track">#FF34322F</color>
    <color name="p1_chooser_scrim">#99201E1C</color>

    <!-- =====================================================================
         PALETTE 02 — NEUTRAL CHARCOAL / STEEL.

         The coolest of the three: blue leads red by four points, which is what
         separates it from Warm Graphite at a glance without either reading as
